---
/** Track data cleaning guide — how GlideComp repairs GPS altitude glitches
 * before analysis, and how every repair is disclosed. Sibling of the GAP and
 * open-distance scoring pages. The example figures are generated from real
 * archive tracklogs by web/scripts/generate-data-cleaning-charts.ts. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BackLink from "../../components/BackLink.astro";
import DataCleaningCharts from "../../components/DataCleaningCharts.astro";
import DataCleaningChartsSynthetic from "../../components/DataCleaningChartsSynthetic.astro";
---

<Base
  title="GlideComp - How Track Data Cleaning Works"
  description="How GlideComp detects and repairs GPS altitude glitches in tracklogs — cross-checking the barometric channel, physical plausibility limits, and full disclosure of every repair."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground py-6 px-page-safe">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <BackLink href="/scoring" label="Scoring" />
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold flex items-center gap-3">
          <img src="/icon.svg" alt="GlideComp logo" class="w-8 h-8" />
          How Track Data Cleaning Works
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">
          Repairing GPS altitude glitches before analysis — and telling you exactly what was
          touched
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* Table of Contents */}
      <nav class="mb-10 p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Contents
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li>
            <a href="#why-clean" class="hover:underline">Why Tracklogs Need Cleaning</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#two-channels" class="hover:underline">The Two Altitude Channels</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#cross-check" class="hover:underline">The Cross-Check: Barometer as Witness</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#rate-limits" class="hover:underline">The Fallback: Physical Plausibility</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#illustrated" class="hover:underline">Error or Real Flying? Illustrated</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#examples" class="hover:underline">Real Examples</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#what-changes" class="hover:underline">What Gets Repaired — and What Never Changes</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#disclosure" class="hover:underline">Full Disclosure on the Report Card</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      {/* Why */}
      <section id="why-clean" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Why Tracklogs Need Cleaning</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Flight recorders sometimes log an altitude no aircraft could fly. It happens when a
            GPS receiver reacquires satellites after being shaded, when its signal reflects off
            nearby terrain, or during a brief dropout. The fix lands hundreds of metres above or
            below the glider, or at exactly zero, which is how the IGC format says "no GPS
            altitude for this fix".
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Across the 3.7 million fixes in the real competition tracklogs GlideComp is tested
            against, brief GPS altitude glitches outnumber zero-dropouts by about 80 to 1. A
            glitch jumps faster than any glider can climb or sink, then returns to the true
            altitude within a few seconds. Left in, a single glitch reads as a 400&nbsp;m climb
            or dive that never happened, and it skews climb rates, glide ratios, thermal
            detection and altitude-band statistics.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Treating every zero as "missing" doesn't work either, because pilots do fly at sea
            level and below it. The cleaning has to tell a measurement error from a real
            altitude, whatever the number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside
          aria-label="Flying at and below sea level"
          class="mt-6 p-5 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card"
        >
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">
            Yes, pilots really do fly at 0&nbsp;m — and below
          </h3>
          <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            <p>
              Flying <em>at</em> zero is routine at every coastal ridge-soaring site. Pilots skim
              a few metres above the water at the Torrey Pines cliffs in San Diego, the Danish
              and Dutch dunes, Wilderness in South Africa, and Australia's soaring beaches. At
              Stanwell Park's Bald Hill, the birthplace of Australian hang gliding, the beach
              landing is at 0&nbsp;m by definition.
            </p>
            <p>
              Flying below zero is real too. The <strong class="text-foreground">Dead
              Sea</strong> shoreline, at about −430&nbsp;m, is the lowest dry land on Earth, and
              paragliders flying the Judean Desert escarpment beside it log negative altitudes
              for a whole descent. Death Valley's Badwater Basin (−86&nbsp;m) sits beside famous
              US hang gliding country, and there is soaring around the edges of California's
              Salton Sea basin (about −70&nbsp;m). Australia's own lowest point,
              <strong class="text-foreground">Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre</strong> (about −15&nbsp;m),
              means an outback cross-country flight can legitimately log a negative altitude.
            </p>
            <p>
              So GlideComp never treats an altitude as wrong because of its value alone. It
              repairs a fix only when physics shows it can't be real: either the barometer
              disagrees, or the climb or sink rate is beyond what any glider can do.
            </p>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </section>

      {/* Two channels */}
      <section id="two-channels" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Two Altitude Channels</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Every fix in an IGC tracklog carries <strong class="text-foreground">two</strong>
            altitudes: the GPS (GNSS) altitude, and a barometric altitude from the instrument's
            pressure sensor. They measure the same flight by different physics, so they differ
            by an offset set by the day's air pressure, the temperature profile and the local
            geoid. That offset is usually a few tens of metres, more at height on a hot day.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            What matters is that the offset changes <em>slowly</em>: by less than ten metres in
            any minute of flight. That makes each channel a reliable witness for the other.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Cross-check */}
      <section id="cross-check" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Cross-Check: Barometer as Witness</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            When the recorder writes both channels, GlideComp tracks the GPS-minus-barometric
            difference through the flight as a rolling two-minute median. A fix whose difference
            strays more than 150&nbsp;m from that baseline is flagged as a GPS error. Its
            altitude is repaired to the barometric reading plus the current offset, which is the
            best available estimate of where the glider really was.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            This is what separates a glitch from real flying. In a genuine fast descent, such as
            a spiral dive, big ears or strong sink, <em>both</em> channels drop together, the
            difference stays flat, and nothing is flagged. On a slow glide to a beach landing at
            0&nbsp;m, both channels agree all the way down, so a real sea-level fix is kept
            exactly as logged. Only a jump in one channel but not the other is an error, and a
            zero-dropout is the extreme case of that.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Rate limits */}
      <section id="rate-limits" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Fallback: Physical Plausibility</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Some instruments record only one altitude channel, such as a GPS-only tracker or a
            barometer-only logger. With no second channel to cross-check against, GlideComp falls
            back on physics. Hang gliders and paragliders can't sustain more than about
            25&nbsp;m/s vertically, even in a spiral dive, so a step of more than 40&nbsp;m/s
            between fixes is suspect.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A suspect step is repaired only if the track <em>returns</em> to near its earlier
            altitude within 30 seconds. That out-and-back shape is the signature of a glitch,
            and GlideComp bridges it with a straight line. If the track never comes back, as
            after a logger restart during the retrieve, nothing is changed, because a repair
            there would invent data.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A slow descent through zero and back up, which is normal at a coastal site, never
            trips this rule, because no single step is large.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Synthetic illustrations of the cross-check */}
      <section id="illustrated" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Error or Real Flying? Illustrated</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Here are the three situations the cleaner has to tell apart, side by side. They use
            <strong class="text-foreground">synthetic data</strong>, and are labelled as such,
            because no track in GlideComp's archive can show the barometer cross-check: scoring
            servers strip the barometric channel on export, as the real examples below show.
            Every line is still the output of the <em>real cleaning engine</em>, and the chart
            generator fails if the engine stops behaving as the captions describe.
          </p>
        </div>
        <DataCleaningChartsSynthetic />
      </section>

      {/* Real examples */}
      <section id="examples" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Real Examples</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-6">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            These come from real competition tracklogs in GlideComp's test archive, each a
            stretch where the cleaning found something to repair. The green band marks the
            repaired fixes. Everywhere else, the cleaned line sits exactly on the raw GPS line.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            They also show a limitation. A scoring server that re-exports a tracklog often strips
            the barometric channel, which then reads 0 for the whole flight, as it does here. On
            such a file the cross-check can't run, so the vertical-speed rule does the work. A
            track uploaded straight from the instrument keeps both channels and gets the
            stronger barometer cross-check.
          </p>
        </div>
        <DataCleaningCharts />
      </section>

      {/* What changes */}
      <section id="what-changes" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What Gets Repaired — and What Never Changes</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Cleaning changes <strong class="text-foreground">altitude only</strong>. It never
            touches positions, times or the tracklog's validity flags. The original file is
            stored and shown unchanged, with both raw altitude channels. Repairs go into a
            separate cleaned altitude series, which is what the analysis reads.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            GAP and open-distance scoring run on positions and times, so cleaning almost never
            changes a score. It matters for everything built on altitude:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-muted-foreground space-y-1 ml-2">
            <li>climb rates and thermal detection</li>
            <li>glide ratios</li>
            <li>take-off and landing detection</li>
            <li>the goal-altitude bonus</li>
            <li>the behavioural comp and task analysis</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Disclosure */}
      <section id="disclosure" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Full Disclosure on the Report Card</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Cleaning is never silent. If any fix in a track was repaired, that pilot's report
            card shows a <strong class="text-foreground">Track data cleaning</strong> note: how
            many fixes were repaired, when each incident happened, how big the correction was,
            and which test flagged it. Most tracks are clean, and a track with no repairs shows
            nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            It's the same transparency rule the rest of GlideComp's scoring follows: every
            number on a leaderboard must be explainable, and everything done on the way to it
            must be visible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
